{"kind": "report", "step": 15, "ts": "2026-11-19T01:39:18.883530+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "The rate limiter on dns1.lan's primary-dns is configured at only 20 q/s aggregated over the whole client subnet (ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24), so ordinary traffic bursts from the 10.20.0.0/24 clients exceed the bucket and queries are silently dropped; during those windows the fallback server (gw.lan) cannot help either, because its unbound forwards .lan to dns1 and then strips the private answer via private-address: 10.0.0.0/8, so getaddrinfo returns ENOTFOUND until the burst passes.", "evidence": ["journalctl -u primary-dns shows exactly three episodes a day matching the alert frequency: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/8 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02, each followed within ~30s by 'back under limit' \u2014 transient, self-clearing.", "/opt/primary-dns/config.yaml sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24, i.e. all LAN clients share one 20 q/s bucket, while internet names keep resolving because they come from the DoT upstream and only local rewrites (*.lan / nas.lan -> 10.20.0.50) go through the filtered path.", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan normally succeeds (nas.lan A 10.20.0.50), confirming the record itself is fine \u2014 failures are intermittent drops, not missing data.", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan and drill on gw.lan return NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 for nas.lan, while drill nas.lan @10.20.0.3 returns the answer \u2014 the fallback server yields no usable address.", "/var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan forwards name lan. to 10.20.0.3 and applies private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 (DNS-rebinding protection), which suppresses the 10.20.0.x answers for .lan, so the second configured resolver can never serve local names.", "mon.lan /etc/resolv.conf lists nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1, so a dropped query at dns1 falls through to a server that always returns an empty answer for .lan."], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise or remove the rate limit on dns1.lan's primary-dns so it fits normal LAN traffic \u2014 e.g. increase ratelimit well above observed bursts and/or set ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 to 32 so each client gets its own bucket instead of the whole /24 sharing 20 q/s. Additionally, exempt the .lan zone from the private-address filtering on gw.lan (e.g. declare the local names as local-data/local-zone served directly rather than forwarded+filtered) so the fallback resolver can still answer local names during any future drop. No change is needed for internet lookups.", "detail": "Aggressive per-/24 ratelimiter on primary-dns drops bursts of LAN queries", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
{"kind": "end", "step": 15, "ts": "2026-11-19T01:39:18.884162+00:00", "data": {"stop_reason": "report", "steps": 15, "denied": 1, "tool_errors": 0}}
